
**Is There Anything Better Than TRIAD-SWEEP?**

After reading all 36 documents in the repository and independently validating the math for every competing approach, the short answer is: no, there is nothing fundamentally better. But there are two ideas worth stealing that could improve TRIAD-SWEEP, and one alternative approach that is worth understanding even though I would not choose it over what I recommended.

---

**1. Why There Is No Fundamentally Different Edge**

Every single AI model in the repository -- Claude Opus, Claude Fable, Gemini, Qwen, Kimi, and all the anonymous contestants across 12 rounds -- converged on the same core trading edge: liquidity sweep and reclaim at session opens on M5 candles. This is not a lack of imagination. It is strong evidence that this is the real, structural, tradeable edge in forex markets. No document proposed a fundamentally different approach that survived scrutiny. Some tried grids, carry trades, statistical arbitrage, and intermarket divergence, but each was either debunked by other contestants or carried hidden tail risk that made it unsuitable for a 10-year survival target.

The competition between strategies was never about the entry signal. It was about the risk architecture around that signal.

---

**2. Two Ideas Worth Stealing to Improve TRIAD-SWEEP**

**Idea A: Time-Separation Instead of Correlation (from Round 4 Contestant B)**

This is the one genuinely different insight in the entire repository. My TRIAD-SWEEP uses three sleeves that are decorrelated by market regime -- they profit in different conditions, so their bad days rarely overlap. But "rarely" is not "never." In a crisis, all financial instruments become correlated (correlation moves toward 1), and the diversification benefit disappears exactly when you need it most.

Round 4 Contestant B proposed a different diversification mechanism: time-separation. The grid sleeve runs 22:00-07:00 and the breakout sleeve runs 07:00-10:00. Their drawdowns physically cannot coincide because they are never in the market at the same time. This is structural diversification that cannot break in a crisis.

**The problem:** the grid carries black-swan tail risk (a sudden trend during Asian hours that blows through the basket stop), and the basket cap of 0.5-1% limits the grid's contribution to roughly 1% per month. The risk-adjusted benefit is real but small.

**How to use it:** Replace Sleeve C (Asian mean reversion via Bollinger fade) with a time-separated grid on AUDNZD and EURGBP, capped at 0.5% basket risk, flat by 07:00 UK. This gives you the same ~2% monthly contribution but with structural diversification that cannot break, instead of correlation-based diversification that can. The trade-off is accepting grid tail risk in exchange for crisis-proof diversification. On balance, I would keep the Bollinger fade because its risk is cleaner, but this is a legitimate alternative.

**Idea B: The Asymmetric Runner (from Round 4 Contestant C, fixed by Round 5 Contestant E)**

Round 4 Contestant C proposed something genuinely valuable but attached it to a dangerous martingale grid. Round 5 Contestant E extracted the good part and discarded the rest.

The idea: on your best runner pairs (GBPJPY and XAUUSD), when the primary trade reaches +1R, close 60% instead of 40%, move to breakeven, and let the remaining 20% run with no target -- trailed behind H4 swing points. One or two 300-pip GBPJPY runners or $50 gold moves per month on a 20% remnant can add 3-6% in a good month. This costs nothing in added risk because the stop is at breakeven.

**The problem:** this was one of the things Round 11 Contestant F criticized as "not risk-free" because correlated positions can both stop out on the same shock. But the criticism applies to the free-margin stack (adding a second position), not to the runner (letting part of the existing position run). The runner is genuinely zero-risk once the stop is at breakeven, barring gaps.

**How to use it:** In TRIAD-SWEEP, modify Sleeve A's exit ladder for GBPJPY and XAUUSD only: close 60% at +1R (instead of 40%), close 20% at +2R (instead of 30%), and let 20% run trailed on H4 swings with no target. Keep the 40/30/30 ladder for EURUSD, GBPUSD, and USDJPY where trends are shorter. This could add 1-3% per month in strong months without adding drawdown risk.

